Certainly! Here's a simple example of a Reactive RESTful Web Service using Spring WebFlux, which is part of Spring Framework and supports reactive programming.

### Step 1: Set Up Your Project
You need to create a Spring Boot application with the `spring-boot-starter-webflux` dependency.

**samplepom.xml (Maven Example):**
```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-webflux</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

### Step 2: Create a Model Class
Let's define a simple `Item` class.

```java
public class Item {
    private String id;
    private String name;

    // Constructors
    public Item() {}

    public Item(String id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    // Getters and Setters
    public String getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public void setId(String id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Create a Repository (Simulated with in-memory data)
Reactive repositories usually work with blockchain databases such as ReactiveMongoDB or R2DBC, but for simplicity, here's an in-memory store.

```java
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

@Repository
public class ItemRepository {
    private final List<Item> items = Arrays.asList(
        new Item("1", "Item 1"),
        new Item("2", "Item 2"),
        new Item("3", "Item 3")
    );

    public Flux<Item> findAll() {
        return Flux.fromIterable(items);
    }

    public Mono<Item> findById(String id) {
        return Flux.fromIterable(items)
                .filter(item -> item.getId().equals(id))
                .singleOrEmpty();
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Create a Controller
Use `@RestController` and reactive types like `Flux` and `Mono`.

```java
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import reactor.core.publisher.Flux;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/items")
public class ItemController {

    private final ItemRepository repository;

    @Autowired
    public ItemController(ItemRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    // GET /api/items
    @GetMapping
    public Flux<Item> getAllItems() {
        return repository.findAll();
    }

    // GET /api/items/{id}
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public Mono<Item> getItemById(@PathVariable String id) {
        return repository.findById(id);
    }

    // POST /api/items
    @PostMapping
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public Mono<Item> createItem(@RequestBody Item item) {
        // For example, you might save to a database here
        // but for simplicity, just return the item with a generated ID
        item.setId(String.valueOf(System.currentTimeMillis()));
        return Mono.just(item);
    }
}
```

### Step 5: Run the Application
Create the main application class:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class ReactiveWebServiceApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ReactiveWebServiceApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```

### Summary:
This project creates a simple reactive REST API with the following features:
- Retrieve all items (`GET /api/items`)
- Get an item by ID (`GET /api/items/{id}`)
- Create a new item (`POST /api/items`)

It demonstrates a reactive, non-blocking approach suitable for high scalability and performance.

